46:(ip30stallgreen).
47:================================================================================
48:THE CONTRACT -- vacuity-floor clause (e).  A detector that says what it catches is
49:FALSIFIABLE; one that claims completeness is not.  Every CATCH below has a MEASURED
50:kill in regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/killtable{,2}_20260917/.
51:================================================================================
52:
53:CATCHES:
54:  * reinstating any of the four abort() calls                (the pre-fix red, 13 rows)
55:  * deleting the announce at ANY of the SIX announce sites   (M3,M4,M7,M8 + the rd txt rows)
56:  * deleting either surviving store (0x10020, 0x10028)       (isr keep, isr clr / M1)
57:  * re-inserting the dropped 0x10030 clear-on-write          (isr drop 30 / M2)
58:  * moving the tick guard between bits 49 and 50             (tick 50 txt + tick 49 / M5)
59:  * mirroring d->isr into a DECLINED read, AT BOTH READ ARMS  (isr 20 rd val / M10,
60:                                                             isr 28 rd val / M11)
61:  * collapsing the two latch bits onto one, IN THE TICK->ISR DIRECTION ONLY
62:                                                             (xbit isr / M6)
63:  * defeating the latch mask outright AT THE 0x10020-READ SITE ONLY
64:                                                             (latch 1x / M9)
65:
66:*** THOSE TWO LINES ONCE READ WITHOUT THEIR QUALIFIERS, AND THE opus5 F2 SEAT MEASURED BOTH
67:UNQUALIFIED CLAIMS FALSE -- EIGHT MUTANTS, ALL 27/27, SIX INSIDE CELLS THIS CONTRACT CALLED
68:CLOSED.  Corrected 2026-09-18 on the fable5 adjudication. ***
69:
70:THE LATCH IS NOT A SHARED RESOURCE WITH TWO FAILURE MODES.  It is SIX DUPLICATED STATEMENTS,
71:one `d->warned |= IP30_W_*' beside every announce -- the same duplication the ANNOUNCE axis was
72:decomposed for.  The real space is {6 sites} x {announce fires, latch sets} = twelve cells, plus
73:{2 collision directions} = FOURTEEN.  THIS DETECTOR ROWS SIX OF THEM.
74:
75:  * `latch 1x' visits 0x10020-RD then 0x10028-RD.  The FIRST access latches, so the SECOND
76:    arm's latch-set is never load-bearing: MF_nolatchset28rd deletes the latch set in an arm
77:    this row literally traverses and scores 27/27.
78:  * `xbit' runs TICK then ISR -- the OPPOSITE order from the hazard the product comment names
79:    ("a guest write to Set-ISR would silence the TIMER diagnostic forever").  Seeing that needs
80:    tick -> ISR -> tick.  MA_assign20wr and MD_assigntick are both 27/27.
81:
82:MEASURED ESCAPES, ALL 27/27, ranked by what they admit (regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/
83:opusF2_20260918/):
84:  ME_nolatchsettick  WORST -- one guest `sd' arming imask0 bit 50 gives AN ANNOUNCE PER TICK,
85:                     FOREVER: guest-drivable floodclass on the arm this round de-fatalised
86:  MA_assign20wr      `|=' -> `=' at 0x10020-WR: one character, falsifies the two-bit design
87:  MD_assigntick      the same at TICK, the other collision direction
88:  MB/MF/MG           delete the latch SET at 0x10020-WR / 0x10028-RD / 0x10030-WR: bounded
89:                     per-arm floods
90:  MH_nocounter       delete `d->reg_0x20000 += 1000': COVERAGE DEBT, not a floor cell -- the
91:                     counter is UNCONDITIONAL, above the de-fatalised guard, so it is
92:                     fix-independent (fable5 ruling)
93:  MC_len4            `&& len == 4' on the 0x10020 write guard: the WIDTH axis, disclosed below
94:
95:*** THE CLOSURE IS OWED IN-ROUND, NOT FILED (fable5, 2026-09-18): six of the eight escapes
96:weaken statements THE FIX'S OWN DIFF INTRODUCED, which is the canonical clause-(d) shape, and
97:the vacuity floor makes that L2 -- replace in-round however late it is found. ***
98:
99:DOES NOT CATCH -- disclosed here rather than discovered later:
100:  * THE WIDTH / len AXIS AT 0x10020 -- *** MEASURED TO ESCAPE, not merely unswept. ***
101:    `V8_len4' (`&& len == 4' on the 0x10020 write guard) scored 33/33 against this instrument
102:    on 2026-09-18.  Ruled OUTSIDE THE FLOOR by two seats: the guarded body never tests `len',
103:    so by the pcc2widthkills bound it is a single-operand MARGINAL -- clause (e) debt.  Carried
104:    with the cost NAMED rather than hidden: `len' takes at most four values and this probe
105:    already has guest_store64/guest_load64, so the debt is CHEAP, not unbounded.
106:    *** A DISCLOSURE BACKED BY A MEASUREMENT TELLS A READER WHICH EDIT WALKS FREE; ONE BACKED BY
107:    AN OMISSION ONLY TELLS THEM NOBODY LOOKED. ***
108:    (A first attempt at this mutant was MIS-BUILT -- anchored on the first `writeflag' guard in
109:    the file, which is `case 0x10000', the INTERRUPT MASK REGISTER.  It killed four tick rows
110:    because they arm imask0 with a 64-bit `sd', and it would have been filed as coverage of a
111:    cell it never touched.  Incidental finding, kept: THIS DETECTOR DOES CATCH A WIDTH NARROWING
112:    AT imask0, with a four-row signature.)
113:  * POSITION / TRANSLATION (clause (f)).  No row asserts WHICH cell an access lands on, so a
114:    re-map that keeps the probed offsets distinct is invisible to every row here.
115:  * latch-macro RENUMBERING -- measured EQUIVALENT (all uses go through the macros and no
116:    observable differs), so a RESULT rather than a gap.
117:  * DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_ip30_2) -- a different device; filed as `ip30asdvasdvnb'.
118:
119:*** THE ATTRIBUTION RULE, WITHOUT WHICH HALF THESE ROWS ARE VACUOUS AND LOOK FINE: ***
120:All FIVE ISR sites print ONE IDENTICAL STRING behind ONE SHARED BIT.  So a row can attribute an
121:announce to a SPECIFIC arm ONLY IF its session makes a SINGLE ISR access.  A multi-access
122:session asserts merely "at least one of these arms announced" -- which is exactly how the
123:original `kill 30 txt' sat GREEN while blind to its own named mutant.  ANY NEW ANNOUNCE ROW
124:MUST USE A SINGLE-ACCESS SESSION.
125:
126:*** AND TWO ROWS ARE COUPLED: `xbit isr' is NOT a pure collision detector. ***  It reds under
127:BOTH the bit-collision mutant (alone) and the 0x10020-announce-deletion mutant (together with
128:`kill 20 wr txt').  Only the PRESENCE of `kill 20 wr txt' separates the two causes.  Delete
129:that row as redundant and `xbit isr' silently stops meaning what its comment says.
130:
131:THE CLAIM UNDER TEST, from reading dev_sgi_ip30.c at HEAD:
132:
133:    :127  case 0x10020  Set ISR    d->isr = idata;  fatal(...); abort();
134:    :135  case 0x10028  Clear ISR  d->isr &= ~idata; fatal(...); abort();
135:    :145  case 0x10030  ISR write arm             fatal(...); abort();
136:    :78   DEVICE_TICK              if (d->imask0 & (1<<50)) { fatal(...); abort(); }
137:
138:`abort()` contains no `exit(', which is why three `exit(' sweeps of this queue walked past all
139:four.  *** AND `case 0x10020' HAS NO `writeflag' TEST AND RUNS `d->isr = idata' FIRST, with
140:idata initialised 0 and assigned only on writes -- so a guest LOAD is predicted to ZERO THE ISR
141:and THEN abort. ***  That asymmetry is why `kill 20 rd' and `kill 30 rd' are separate rows:
142:0x10030 IS writeflag-tested and its READ is predicted to be SERVICED.
143:
144:WITNESS-LADDER CLAUSE (ii) -- rung 3 owes BOTH controls, because one is not enough (the
145:footbridge probe's first draft returned 0x0 everywhere WITH ITS RAM CONTROL GREEN):
146:
147:    live    LIVENESS   -- a known value planted in RAM returns through a guest load
148:    devsig  DEVICE-SIG -- case 0x20 returns 0x80010000, a value RAM CANNOT produce
149:                          (dev_sgi_ip30.c `case 0x20:  odata = 0x80010000ULL;')
150:
151:Clause (iii): the machine description and dispatch are COMMITTED AND UNMODIFIED.  No device_add
152:is introduced to reach the site -- `machine_sgi.c:417' already adds `sgi_ip30 addr=0x0ff00000'
153:and `:744' advertises the subtype.
154:
155:POLARITY: **WANT IS POST-FIX CORRECT**, the house convention -- so a kill row wants `alive' and
156:its PRE-FIX FAIL *IS* the reproduction.  Written want=DEAD first, which scored a tidy 9/9 on the
157:defect and would have gone RED after a correct fix, reading as though the fix broke something.
158:
159:*** ORACLE POLARITY -- EOF ALONE IS THE KILL. ***  The text is DIAGNOSIS, not half the trigger.
160:A build that still abort()s with a REWORDED message gives EOF and no matching text; an
161:EOF-AND-text oracle would never fire and would certify a live defect GREEN.  That lesson was
162:bought on the ARM MSR spec this same day and is applied here rather than re-learned.
163:"""
164:import os
165:import pty
166:import re
167:import select
168:import signal
169:import sys
170:import time
171:
172:BIN = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else "../../../build/gxemul"
173:LABEL = sys.argv[2] if len(sys.argv) > 2 else "?"
174:
175:#  The stub is a GENERATED artefact, never committed (top-level regress/*.bin is NEVER STAGE).
176:#  All 11 top-level probes that reference such a stub create it if absent; this does the same,
177:#  so the probe runs on a fresh clone.
178:DUMMY = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "ip30_dummy.bin")
179:if not os.path.exists(DUMMY):
180:    with open(DUMMY, "wb") as f:
181:        f.write(b"\0" * 8)
182:
183:CODE = 0x80001000
184:SENTINEL = 0x5a5a5a5a
185:RAMPLANT = 0x11223344
186:
187:#  KSEG1 (uncached), sign-extended the way the file's own :113 comment prints it:
188:#  "Address: 0xffffffffaff10018".  base 0x0ff00000 -> 0xffffffffaff00000.
189:BASE = 0xFFFFFFFFAFF00000
190:A_BANKCFG = BASE + 0x00020     # case 0x20    -> 0x80010000, the DEVICE SIGNATURE
191:A_IMASK0 = BASE + 0x10000      # case 0x10000 -> implemented r/w
192:A_R10018 = BASE + 0x10018      # case 0x10018 -> implemented r/w
193:A_SETISR = BASE + 0x10020      # case 0x10020 -> PREDICTED KILL, and no writeflag test
194:A_CLRISR = BASE + 0x10028      # case 0x10028 -> PREDICTED KILL, and no writeflag test
195:A_ISR = BASE + 0x10030         # case 0x10030 -> writeflag-TESTED; READ predicted SERVICED
196:A_C20000 = BASE + 0x20000    # case 0x20000 -> implemented r/w counter
197:A_C30000 = BASE + 0x30000    # case 0x30000 -> implemented r/w
198:A_RAM = 0xFFFFFFFF80002000     # KSEG0 RAM, for the liveness control
199:
200:OP_LW_V0_AT = 0x8C220000       # lw   $v0, 0($at)
201:OP_SW_T0_A3 = 0xACE80000       # sw   $t0, 0($a3)
202:#  *** 64-BIT FORMS ARE NOT OPTIONAL FOR imask0.  `case 0x10000' does
203:#  `d->imask0 = idata' with `idata = memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len)', so a 4-BYTE
204:#  `sw' CAPS idata BELOW 2^32 AND BIT 50 CAN NEVER BE SET.  Measured: sw 0xffffffff
205:#  + 70000 steps -> ALIVE.  A tick row built with OP_SW_T0_A3 would arm NOTHING,
206:#  stay alive, and score GREEN ON A LIVE HOST DEATH. ***
207:OP_SD_T0_A3 = 0xFCE80000       # sd   $t0, 0($a3)   -- REQUIRED to reach bit 50
208:OP_LD_V0_AT = 0xDC220000       # ld   $v0, 0($at)   -- its readback
209:OP_B_SELF = 0x1000FFFF         # b    .
210:OP_NOP = 0x00000000
211:
212:rows = []
213:
214:
215:def row(name, got, want, note=""):
216:    rows.append((name, str(got), str(want), note))
217:
218:
219:def session(cmds, settle=2.0, spin=2.5):
220:    """One fresh process per SESSION -- most sessions score one row, but
221:    `tick 50`/`tick 50 txt` and `xbit isr`/`xbit tick` each score two rows from
222:    one process, by design (the cross-bit mutant needs both announce classes in
223:    one device lifetime) (#561).  Returns (transcript, alive)."""
224:    pid, fd = pty.fork()
225:    if pid == 0:
226:        os.execv(BIN, [BIN, "-V", "-x", "-E", "sgi", "-e", "ip30", "-M", "64",
227:                       "0x%x:" % CODE + DUMMY])
228:        os._exit(127)
229:    buf = ""
230:
231:    def rd(t):
232:        nonlocal buf
233:        end = time.time() + t
234:        while time.time() < end:
235:            try:
236:                r, _, _ = select.select([fd], [], [], 0.1)
237:            except OSError:
238:                return
239:            if not r:
240:                continue
241:            try:
242:                d = os.read(fd, 65536)
243:            except OSError:
244:                return
245:            if not d:
246:                return
247:            buf += d.decode("utf-8", "replace")
248:
249:    rd(settle)
250:    for c in cmds:
251:        try:
252:            os.write(fd, (c + "\n").encode())
253:        except OSError:
254:            break
255:        rd(0.35)
256:    rd(spin)
257:
258:    #  *** POLL, NEVER SAMPLE ONCE.  MEASURED 2026-09-17, and the first run of this probe
259:    #  reported `alive' for a process that SIGABRT'd 0.6 s later. ***
260:    #  `rd()' RETURNS IMMEDIATELY ON EOF rather than waiting out its timeout, so `spin' --
261:    #  which exists precisely to give a dying child time to die -- is SKIPPED EXACTLY WHEN THE
262:    #  CHILD DIES.  A single waitpid(WNOHANG) then lands ~0 s after EOF while the kernel is
263:    #  still writing a 6 MB core, reports "still running", and THE KILL ROW GOES GREEN ON A
264:    #  LIVE HOST DEATH.  That is the false-green this probe exists to refuse.
265:    alive = True
266:    deadline = time.time() + 12.0
267:    while time.time() < deadline:
268:        try:
269:            wpid, status = os.waitpid(pid, os.WNOHANG)
270:        except ChildProcessError:
271:            alive = False
272:            break
273:        if wpid == pid:
274:            alive = False
275:            break
276:        time.sleep(0.05)
277:    if alive:
278:        try:
279:            os.kill(pid, signal.SIGKILL)
280:            os.waitpid(pid, 0)
281:        except OSError:
282:            pass
283:    try:
284:        os.close(fd)
285:    except OSError:
286:        pass
287:    return buf, alive
288:
289:
290:def run_ops(ops, at=CODE):
291:    put = ["put w 0x%x, 0x%08x" % (at + 4 * i, op) for i, op in enumerate(ops)]
292:    put += ["put w 0x%x, 0x%08x" % (at + 4 * len(ops), OP_B_SELF),
293:            "put w 0x%x, 0x%08x" % (at + 4 * len(ops) + 4, OP_NOP)]
294:    return put + ["pc=0x%x" % at, "step %d" % len(ops)]
295:
296:
297:def guest_load(addr):
298:    """A REAL guest instruction through real decode and real memory_rw -- rung 3.
299:    NOT `dump': all four ip30 registrations are DM_DEFAULT, so dump BYPASSES the handler
300:    and would prove nothing about the device at all."""
301:    return ["at=0x%x" % addr, "v0=0x%x" % SENTINEL] + run_ops([OP_LW_V0_AT]) + ["print v0"]
302:
303:
304:def guest_store(addr, val):
305:    return ["a3=0x%x" % addr, "t0=0x%x" % val] + run_ops([OP_SW_T0_A3])
306:
307:
308:def guest_store64(addr, val):
309:    """A 64-BIT store.  The ONLY way to reach imask0 bit 50 -- see OP_SD_T0_A3."""
310:    return ["a3=0x%x" % addr, "t0=0x%x" % val] + run_ops([OP_SD_T0_A3])
311:
312:
313:def guest_load64(addr):
314:    return ["at=0x%x" % addr, "v0=0x%x" % SENTINEL] + run_ops([OP_LD_V0_AT]) + ["print v0"]
315:
316:
317:def printed_last(t, reg):
318:    """*** THE LAST `print <reg>', NOT THE FIRST. ***  L1 repair 2026-09-17.
319:
320:    `printed()' uses `re.search', which returns the FIRST match.  A row that issues
321:    MORE THAN ONE guest_load therefore reads the WRONG load's value, silently -- and
322:    `isr keep' does three commands ending in the read-back it actually wants.
323:
324:    PRE-FIX THE BUG WAS INVISIBLE because the row's seeding write aborted and the row
325:    read `died'; the construction defect only surfaced once the fix let the session
326:    survive.  A row that cannot pass even on a correct fix is vacuous, so this is L1
327:    construction, repaired in-round rather than filed."""
328:    m = re.findall(r"print\s+%s\s*\n\s*(0x[0-9a-f]+)" % reg, t)
329:    return m[-1] if m else "ABSENT"
330:
331:
332:def counted(t, needle):
333:    """*** HOW MANY TIMES DID THIS ANNOUNCE FIRE? ***  Added by the #531 class closure.
334:
335:    Presence is not enough for the LATCH cells.  A defeated latch (mask -> 0) announces on
336:    EVERY access and a presence oracle reads `yes' either way -- the CHANNEL vacuity, one
337:    level along.  Only a COUNT varies under that mutant."""
338:    return t.count(needle)
339:
340:
341:def printed(t, reg):
342:    """`print <reg>' emits a BARE 0x... -- `reg' is the command that carries the `r0 = ' prefix.
343:    Copied from regress/lca_isalane_probe.py rather than invented."""
344:    m = re.search(r"print\s+%s\s*\n\s*(0x[0-9a-f]+)" % reg, t)
345:    return m.group(1) if m else "ABSENT"
346:
347:
348:# ------------------------------------------------------------------ CONTROL=DEAD set
349:#  If any of these reds the vehicle is not the vehicle, and NO kill row may be scored.
350:t, alive = session(["put w 0x%x, 0x%08x" % (A_RAM & 0xFFFFFFFF, RAMPLANT)]
351:                   + guest_load(A_RAM))
352:live_got = printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died"
353:row("live", live_got, "0x%x" % RAMPLANT,
354:    "LIVENESS: a known value planted in RAM returns through a guest load. If this reds, plant "
355:    "or step or print never executed and nothing below is evidence.")
356:
357:t, alive = session(guest_load(A_BANKCFG))
358:devsig_got = printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died"
359:row("devsig", devsig_got, "0xffffffff80010000",
360:    "DEVICE-SIGNATURE: case 0x20 answers 0x80010000 and `lw' SIGN-EXTENDS it to "
361:    "0xffffffff80010000 on this 64-bit R10000 -- a value RAM cannot produce. One control is "
362:    "NOT enough: the footbridge probe's first draft returned 0x0 everywhere with its RAM "
363:    "control green.")
364:
365:# ------------------------------------------------------------------ accept side
366:t, alive = session(guest_store(A_R10018, 0xDEADBEEF) + guest_load(A_R10018))
367:row("acc 18", (printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died"), "0xffffffffdeadbeef",
368:    "case 0x10018 is IMPLEMENTED r/w (the PROM complains without it). An accept control, and "
369:    "the de-escalation clause's accept row for this arm.  *** THE WANT IS SIGN-EXTENDED: `lw' sign-extends on this 64-bit R10000, so 0xdeadbeef comes back as 0xffffffffdeadbeef.  My first oracle wanted the truncated form and reddened a CORRECT device -- the apparatus was wrong, not the model. ***")
370:
371:t, alive = session(guest_load(A_ISR))
372:row("acc 30 rd", ("alive" if alive else "died"), "alive",
373:    "case 0x10030 IS writeflag-tested, so its READ returns d->isr and must NOT die. This is "
374:    "the row that proves the three arms are NOT uniform.")
375:
376:#  ---------------------------------------------------------------- de-escalation accept rows
377:#  A fatal->survive fix DELETES the accidental tripwire that made guard growth self-announcing,
378:#  so the detector shipping WITH the fix owes the ACCEPT SIDE of every predicate that used to
379:#  gate death.  These are owed under EVERY candidate member, which is why they are written
380:#  before the disposition is settled -- they cannot be wasted work.
381:#  *** THIS ROW EXPECTED A COUNTER NOT TO COUNT, SO IT WAS RED ON A CORRECT BUILD.  L1
382:#  construction repair, 2026-09-17, found by running the pre-fix baseline. ***
383:#
384:#  Measured: stored 0x1234, read back 0x161c -- and 0x161c - 0x1234 = 0x3e8 = 1000 EXACTLY,
385:#  which is `d->reg_0x20000 += 1000' in DEVICE_TICK.  The DEVICE was right and the ROW was
386:#  wrong: as written it manufactured a phantom failure on a correct build, so it could never
387:#  signal the defect this probe exists for.  (The file's own `/*  A counter  */' comment on
388:#  case 0x20000 says so; the access arm alone reads like a plain r/w register.)
389:#
390:#  THE REPAIRED ORACLE IS STRICTLY STRONGER THAN THE ONE IT REPLACES.  The counter is always
391:#  a multiple of 1000 (it starts at 0 and only ever += 1000), and 0x1234 = 4660 is NOT a
392:#  multiple of 1000 -- so:
393:#      store LANDED    -> readback - 0x1234 == N*1000, an exact multiple
394:#      store LOST      -> readback is some M*1000, and M*1000 - 4660 is NEVER a multiple
395:#  The row therefore pins THAT THE STORE REACHED reg_0x20000, which the old literal compare
396:#  only did by accident on a tickless run, while tolerating any number of ticks.
397:t, alive = session(guest_store(A_C20000, 0x1234) + guest_load(A_C20000))
398:_v20000 = printed(t, "v0")
399:_got20000 = "ABSENT"
400:if alive and _v20000 != "ABSENT":
401:    _d20000 = int(_v20000, 16) - 0x1234
402:    _got20000 = ("stored+N*1000" if (_d20000 >= 0 and _d20000 % 1000 == 0)
403:                 else _v20000)
404:row("acc 20000", (_got20000 if alive else "died"), "stored+N*1000",
405:    "case 0x20000 is writeflag-tested r/w AND is incremented 1000 per DEVICE_TICK, so the "
406:    "oracle pins that the STORE LANDED (the residue is an exact multiple of the tick stride) "
407:    "rather than that a counter stood still.  A neighbour that must keep working.")
408:
409:t, alive = session(guest_store(A_C30000, 0x5678) + guest_load(A_C30000))
410:row("acc 30000", (printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died"), "0x5678",
411:    "case 0x30000, writeflag-tested r/w. Second neighbour.")
412:
413:#  *** THIS IS **THE** ACCEPT ROW FOR THE TICK, not merely a neighbour: the write to imask0 is
414:#  precisely what used to ARM the host death at :78, so it is the predicate that gated death. ***
415:#  *** THE SENTENCE THAT STOOD HERE WAS MEASURED FALSE THE SAME DAY.  It read: "Safe under
416:#  `step' ONLY: tickshift is 16, so the tick fires every 65536 cycles and a handful of steps
417:#  cannot reach it."  A HANDFUL OF STEPS REACHES IT ON THE FIRST ONE. ***
418:#  `machine_add_tickfunction' sets `ticks_till_next[n] = 0' (machine.c:354) while
419:#  `ticks_reset_value[n] = 1 << tickshift' (:355), and `machine_run()' decrements then fires
420:#  while <= 0 -- so THE FIRST machine_run OF ANY SESSION FIRES EVERY TICK FUNCTION.
421:#  THIS ROW IS STILL SAFE -- BUT ONLY ONE OF THE TWO REASONS BELOW IS LOAD-BEARING, AND
422:#  SAYING OTHERWISE INVERTS A HAZARD INTO A SAFETY PROPERTY (Fable adjudication, same day):
423:#  *** REASON (2) MISDIRECTS.  This row's value is 1<<18 = 0x40000, which has NO high bits
424:#  at ANY store width -- so width is irrelevant to THIS row, and reason (1) alone carries
425:#  it.  Written as a pair, `sw cannot set bit 50' reads as a general safety invariant for
426:#  imask0, AND IT IS NOT ONE: THE DEATH IS REACHABLE, BY `sd 1<<50'. ***
427:#  Keep both facts, but know which is which: (1) is why this row is safe; (2) is why a
428:#  COPY of it would be inert as a kill row.  They are a safety reason and a HAZARD.
429:#    (1) it writes bit 18, not bit 50, so the guarded branch is never armed; and
430:#    (2) *** IT USES `sw', AND A 4-BYTE STORE CANNOT SET BIT 50 AT ALL. ***  `case 0x10000'
431:#        does `d->imask0 = idata' with `idata = memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len)', so a
432:#        word store caps idata below 2^32.  ARMING THE TICK REQUIRES `sd' (0xFCE80000).
433:#  *** AND THAT IS A FALSE-GREEN TRAP FOR THE NEXT PERSON: `OP_SW_T0_A3' IS THE ONLY STORE
434:#  HELPER IN THIS FILE, so a tick row copy-pasted from here would arm NOTHING, stay ALIVE,
435:#  and score GREEN ON A LIVE HOST DEATH. ***  Measured: sw 0xffffffff + 70000 steps -> alive.
436:#  The tick witness lives in tick_20260917/ip30_tick_probe.py, which uses `sd'.
437:t, alive = session(guest_store(A_IMASK0, 1 << 18) + guest_load(A_IMASK0))
438:row("acc imask", (printed(t, "v0") if alive else "died"), "0x40000",
439:    "case 0x10000 (imask0) accepts and reads back. THE accept row for the tick predicate -- "
440:    "bit 18 is used, NOT bit 50, so the row does not arm the death it is pinning.")
441:
442:# ------------------------------------------------------------------ the kill rows
443:#  EOF ALONE => RED.  The text is diagnosis.
444:for nm, addr, note in (
445:    ("kill 20 rd", A_SETISR,
446:     "*** A GUEST **LOAD** AT 0x0ff10020. case 0x10020 has NO writeflag test and runs "
447:     "`d->isr = idata' FIRST with idata==0, so this is predicted to ZERO THE ISR and THEN "
448:     "abort() -- corruption AND host death in one arm. ***"),
449:    ("kill 28 rd", A_CLRISR,
450:     "case 0x10028, same missing writeflag test; `d->isr &= ~idata' is a no-op at idata==0, "
451:     "then abort()."),
452:):
453:    t, alive = session(guest_load(addr))
454:    row(nm, ("alive" if alive else "DEAD"), "alive", note)
455:    #  *** RE-PINNED 2026-09-17 ONTO THE SHIPPED #531 TEXT.  The old literal was the
456:    #  PRE-FIX message `IP30 legacy interrupt rewrite: TODO', which #531 replaces with a
457:    #  latched announce -- so this row pinned a string the fix legitimately renames.
458:    #  ORDER DISCRIMINATOR (the oracle-correction rule): the re-pin is shown RED on a
459:    #  PRE-FIX binary before being trusted -- see artifacts/ip30abortkill/REPIN_*.out.
460:    #  *** WANT "no" SINCE 2026-09-18: the read arms adopted MEMBER 2, SILENT DECLINE, on the
461:    #  fable5 ruling.  These rows previously wanted "yes" and pinned an announce that was a
462:    #  FALSE MESSAGE (a read is not a rewrite) and a BIT THIEF (one benign read consumed the
463:    #  shared IP30_W_ISR latch and silenced the genuine write-side diagnostic for the device's
464:    #  life).  They now pin the SILENCE, which is the repair's own observable. ***
465:    row(nm + " txt", ("yes" if "sgi_ip30: interrupt-status rewrite not implemented" in t else "no"), "no",
466:        "DIAGNOSIS ONLY, never half the trigger: a reworded-message mutant still abort()s, "
467:        "gives EOF with no text, and an EOF-AND-text oracle would certify it GREEN.")
468:
469:t, alive = session(guest_store(A_ISR, 0x1))
470:row("kill 30 wr", ("alive" if alive else "DEAD"), "alive",
471:    "case 0x10030's WRITE arm aborts while its read is serviced -- the asymmetry, measured.")
472:#  Closes M_latch_silent_30: deleting the 0x10030 write-arm fatal() while keeping survival.
473:#
474:#  *** THIS ROW SAT 65 LINES LOWER AND READ `isr keep''s TRANSCRIPT, WHICH WRITES 0x10020 AND
475:#  NEVER TOUCHES 0x10030.  Found by the pass-2 agy seat; L1, repaired in-round. ***  It is the
476:#  THIRD rolling-`t' defect in this one probe, and the one the output could never reveal:
477:#  ALL FIVE ISR announces are BYTE-IDENTICAL, so the row read `yes' from the 0x10020 arm and
478:#  was GREEN while blind to its own named mutant.  The shared message is correct design (see
479:#  the latch comment in dev_sgi_ip30.c) and is exactly what made this invisible.
480:row("kill 30 txt", ("yes" if "sgi_ip30: interrupt-status rewrite not implemented" in t else "no"), "yes",
481:    "DIAGNOSIS sibling for the 0x10030 WRITE arm, bound to THIS session's 0x10030 store.  "
482:    "`kill 20/28 rd txt' never visit that arm, so without this a mutant can delete its announce "
483:    "silently.  Never half a trigger.")
484:
485:#  ------------------------------------------------- rows the PROBE ATTACK proved missing
486:#  Every one of these closes a mutant that scored 12/12 against the probe without it.  They
487:#  are L1 CONSTRUCTION -- found while this is still a draft, which is the whole reason the
488:#  detector was attacked BEFORE the fix shipped.
489:
490:#  *** THE TICK ROW.  Closes M_tick_abort (which is ALSO clause (b) at site 4 -- a VERBATIM
491:#  REINSTATEMENT the probe could not see) and M_tick_bit49. ***  It uses `sd' because `sw'
492:#  cannot set bit 50 at all, and `step 1' suffices because machine_add_tickfunction sets
493:#  ticks_till_next[n] = 0 (machine.c:354), so the FIRST machine_run fires every tick function.
494:#  *** THE CHANNEL, AND WHY SURVIVAL ALONE IS NOT ONE POST-FIX.  ***  The pass-2 codex seat
495:#  measured that after de-fatalisation `tick 50' and `tick 49' BOTH read `alive', so the pair
496:#  that discriminated pre-fix (ALIVE against DEAD) stops discriminating on the shipped build --
497:#  the CHANNEL check's "a row whose named channel cannot vary under its named mutant is vacuous
498:#  by construction", arriving because the fix removed the channel the rows were built on.
499:#  The channel that DOES vary post-fix is the ANNOUNCE, so the pair is re-aimed onto it.
500:TICKMSG = "sgi_ip30: timer interrupt delivery not implemented"
501:ISRMSG  = "sgi_ip30: interrupt-status rewrite not implemented"
502:
503:t, alive = session(guest_store64(A_IMASK0, 1 << 50) + ["step 1"])
504:row("tick 50", ("alive" if alive else "DEAD"), "alive",
505:    "*** ARM imask0 BIT 50 WITH `sd', THEN ONE STEP. ***  Pre-fix the DEVICE_TICK abort fires "
506:    "and the host dies; post-fix it must survive with a latched announce.  `sw' here would arm "
507:    "nothing and score GREEN on a live death -- that is the trap this row exists to avoid.")
508:#  Pre-fix this reads `no': HEAD prints `IP30 legacy interrupt rewrite: TODO' and aborts, so the
509:#  row is RED on the committed build and green only once the fix renames and latches it.
510:row("tick 50 txt", ("yes" if TICKMSG in t else "no"), "yes",
511:    "*** THE POST-FIX HALF OF THE DISCRIMINATOR. ***  `tick 50' pins that the host LIVES; this "
512:    "pins that the arm still ANNOUNCES.  Without it a mutant deleting the tick fatal() leaves "
513:    "both tick rows green, and so does one moving the guard to bit 49.")
514:#  The DISCRIMINATOR: same store, same step, bit 49 instead of 50.  Without it, `tick 50' could
515:#  be passing for any reason at all and M_tick_bit49 walks free.
516:t, alive = session(guest_store64(A_IMASK0, 1 << 49) + ["step 1"])
517:row("tick 49", (("yes" if TICKMSG in t else "no") if alive else "died"), "no",
518:    "DISCRIMINATOR for `tick 50': bit 49 is NOT the guarded bit, so the arm must stay SILENT on "
519:    "BOTH builds.  Survival is still pinned -- a dead host reads `died', not `no'.  Re-aimed "
520:    "from a bare survival oracle, which post-fix could not vary: see the CHANNEL note above.  "
521:    "A mutant moving the guard to 1<<49 makes this announce and reds the row.")
522:
523:#  *** THE WRITE ARMS.  The probe only ever LOADED at 0x10020/0x10028, so M_wf20_abort_wr and
524:#  M_wf28_abort_wr -- abort() kept INSIDE the write arm -- were invisible. ***
525:for nm, addr in (("kill 20 wr", A_SETISR), ("kill 28 wr", A_CLRISR)):
526:    t, alive = session(guest_store(addr, 0x1))
527:    row(nm, ("alive" if alive else "DEAD"), "alive",
528:        "A guest WRITE at this offset.  Pre-fix it reaches the same abort(); post-fix it must "
529:        "survive.  Without this row a fix that adds the writeflag test and leaves abort() in "
530:        "the WRITE arm scores a clean sweep.")
531:    #  *** CLASS CLOSURE.  These two sessions are SINGLE-ACCESS, which is the only shape that
532:    #  can attribute an announce to one arm -- all five ISR sites print one identical string
533:    #  behind one shared bit.  Without these, M9_silent20wr and M5_silent28wr (drop the announce
534:    #  in these very arms) BOTH scored 19/19 against the measuring seat's table. ***
535:    row(nm + " txt", ("yes" if ISRMSG in t else "no"), "yes",
536:        "DIAGNOSIS for the " + nm[5:7] + " WRITE arm specifically.  Single-access session, so a "
537:        "`yes' here can only have come from THIS arm.  Deleting this arm's announce reds it.")
538:
539:#  *** THE asicindexcase ROW.  Closes M_wf20_store_out -- the writeflag wrapping only the
540:#  ANNOUNCE while `d->isr = idata' still runs on a LOAD.  That mutant leaves the host ALIVE and
541:#  the guest's read still zeroes the ISR, which is WORSE than the defect being fixed. ***
542:#  PRE-FIX THIS ROW READS `dead': the seeding write at 0x10020 itself aborts.  That is correct
543:#  and is part of the reproduction, not an apparatus failure.
544:t, alive = session(guest_store(A_SETISR, 0x5a5a)      # seed ISR through the Set arm
545:                   + guest_load(A_SETISR)             # the LOAD that must not clobber it
546:                   + guest_load(A_ISR))               # read it back through the read-only ISR
547:row("isr keep", (printed_last(t, "v0") if alive else "died"), "0x5a5a",
548:    "*** ISR UNCHANGED ACROSS A LOAD AT 0x10020. ***  Seed via the Set arm, load at 0x10020, "
549:    "read back at 0x10030 (heart.h: isr is the read-only status register).  If a fix declines "
550:    "the ANNOUNCE but still runs the store on a read, this row reds while every survival row "
551:    "stays green.  Pre-fix it reads `died' because the seeding write aborts -- that is the "
552:    "reproduction, not a broken row.")
553:
554:#  *** THE 0x10028 STORE IS THE FIX'S REPAIR AT THAT SITE AND NO ROW OBSERVED IT -- a clause-(d)
555:#  CLEAN SWEEP, L2, repaired in-round. ***  Four seats converged independently (deepseek, glm,
556:#  kimi-substitute, minimax): the member-4 ACCEPT at 0x10028 KEEPS `d->isr &= ~idata', and (d)
557:#  says keep the guard and null the repair -- which deletes exactly that line.  `kill 28 wr'
558:#  pins only that the host survives, and a deleted store survives perfectly well.
559:t, alive = session(guest_store(A_SETISR, 0x5a5a)      # seed ISR = 0x5a5a through the Set arm
560:                   + guest_store(A_CLRISR, 0x0a0a)    # clear those bits through the Clear arm
561:                   + guest_load(A_ISR))               # read back through the read-only ISR
562:row("isr clr", (printed_last(t, "v0") if alive else "died"), "0x5050",
563:    "*** 0x5a5a & ~0x0a0a = 0x5050. ***  Pins that the Clear-ISR store STILL RUNS post-fix.  "
564:    "Delete `d->isr &= ~idata' and the readback stays 0x5a5a, reddening this row -- no survival "
565:    "row in the probe can see that edit.  Pre-fix the seeding write aborts, so this reads `died'.")
566:
567:#  *** THE DROPPED CLEAR-ON-WRITE AT 0x10030 -- named by agy, glm and the kimi substitute, and
568:#  the ONLY row that pins it stays dropped. ***  heart.h marks `isr' read-only, so this round
569:#  DELETED the clear.  Re-inserting it is a (b)-adjacent mutant that every survival row passes.
570:t, alive = session(guest_store(A_SETISR, 0x5a5a)      # seed ISR = 0x5a5a
571:                   + guest_store(A_ISR, 0x1234)       # write the READ-ONLY register
572:                   + guest_load(A_ISR))               # it must come back UNTOUCHED
573:row("isr drop 30", (printed_last(t, "v0") if alive else "died"), "0x5a5a",
574:    "*** THE WRITE MUST BE DISCARDED. ***  Re-inserting `d->isr &= ~idata' here gives "
575:    "0x5a5a & ~0x1234 = 0x484a and reds this row.  `kill 30 wr' pins survival only and is "
576:    "structurally blind to it -- the refusal-EFFECT channel, not the refusal.")
577:
578:#  =====================================================================================
579:#  THE LATCH-MECHANISM CELLS.  Every row above asks WHETHER an arm announced; these ask
580:#  HOW MANY TIMES and FROM WHICH BIT.  The measuring seat built two one-character mutants
581:#  that no presence oracle can see, and both scored 19/19.
582:#  =====================================================================================
583:
584:#  *** CROSS-BIT.  Closes the bit-collision mutant (IP30_W_ISR (1<<1) -> (1<<0)). ***
585:#  Recipe MEASURED by the opus5 seat, and its first attempt was a DEFECTIVE APPARATUS worth
586:#  recording: machine_add_tickfunction sets ticks_till_next[n] = 0, so the tick's one free
587:#  firing lands on the session's FIRST machine_run -- a seeding store BEFORE the arm consumes
588:#  it, and the row then says nothing.  ARM BIT 50 FIRST.
589:t, alive = session(guest_store64(A_IMASK0, 1 << 50) + ["step 1"] + guest_store(A_SETISR, 0x1))
590:row("xbit isr", (str(counted(t, ISRMSG)) if alive else "died"), "1",
591:    "*** TICK FIRST, THEN AN ISR ACCESS, IN ONE DEVICE LIFETIME. ***  Both classes must "
592:    "announce, from their OWN bits.  Collapse IP30_W_ISR onto IP30_W_TICK and the tick consumes "
593:    "the bit first, so the ISR arm falls silent and this reads 0.  No fresh-process row can see "
594:    "that mutant at all -- it needs both announce classes in ONE lifetime.")
595:row("xbit tick", (str(counted(t, TICKMSG)) if alive else "died"), "1",
596:    "The other half of the same session: the TICK announce is still exactly one.  Together with "
597:    "`xbit isr' this pins that the two bits are INDEPENDENT, which is the claim the round makes "
598:    "in product code and which nothing measured before now.")
599:
600:#  *** ANNOUNCE COUNT.  Closes the latch-defeat mutant ((1<<1) -> (0<<1)). ***
601:#  A presence oracle reads `yes' whether the latch works or not; only a COUNT varies.
602:#  *** RE-AIMED 2026-09-18 ONTO THE WRITE ARMS, BECAUSE THE F13 SILENT-DECLINE REPAIR DESTROYED
603:#  THE CHANNEL THIS ROW WAS BUILT ON.  Measured: it read got=0 want=1 the moment the read arms
604:#  went silent -- vacuous by construction, exactly as its own CHANNEL note warns. ***
605:#
606:#  THAT IS THE THIRD TIME IN THIS ROUND A FIX HAS REMOVED THE CHANNEL A ROW RESTED ON: the tick
607:#  pair when de-fatalisation removed the DEATH channel, `kill 20/28 rd txt' when the silent
608:#  decline removed the ANNOUNCE, and now this.  The general form is worth more than the fix:
609:#  *a repair does not only have to be correct, it has to be checked against every row whose
610:#  oracle reads the channel it changes* -- and a row so invalidated goes GREEN or RED for a
611:#  reason unrelated to the mutant it names.
612:#
613:#  Two WRITE accesses still announce (0x10020-WR and 0x10028-WR are member-4 ACCEPT arms that
614:#  keep their store AND announce), so the latch-defeat channel survives there.
615:t, alive = session(guest_store(A_SETISR, 0x1) + guest_store(A_CLRISR, 0x1))
616:row("latch 1x", (str(counted(t, ISRMSG)) if alive else "died"), "1",
617:    "TWO declined ISR accesses, ONE announce -- that is what the unqualified `(once)' marker "
618:    "in the message text CLAIMS, so this row is what makes the marker true rather than decorative.  "
619:    "Defeat the latch and it reads 2.")
620:
621:#  *** THE DECLINED READ'S VALUE.  Closes the mirror mutant grok named at design time. ***
622:t, alive = session(guest_store(A_SETISR, 0x5a5a) + guest_load(A_SETISR))
623:row("isr 20 rd val", (printed_last(t, "v0") if alive else "died"), "0x0",
624:    "A declined READ at 0x10020 returns ZERO, not a mirror of d->isr.  `odata = d->isr' here "
625:    "would read 0x5a5a and red this row.  The ISR is seeded NON-ZERO on purpose: against a "
626:    "zero ISR the mutant and the fix are indistinguishable, which is how a value row goes "
627:    "vacuous without looking it.")
628:
629:#  *** SIBLING PARITY -- THE SYMMETRIC CELL, AND ITS ABSENCE WAS A FLOOR BREACH IN THE CLASS
630:#  CLOSURE ITSELF.  Required by the agy seat; grok independently named it and diagnosed the
631:#  cause. ***  The two READ arms are structurally identical -- both decline, both announce,
632:#  both leave odata 0 -- so closing the declined-VALUE cell at 0x10020 and not at 0x10028 is
633:#  the #499 shape exactly: repaired at the address the mutant used, symmetric cell left open.
634:#
635:#  THE CAUSE IS WORTH MORE THAN THE ROW, and it was in the round's own pre-table block:
636:#  PRETABLE.out listed BOTH read arms as "decline, no value to pin".  *** A DECLINE HAS A
637:#  VALUE, AND IT IS 0. ***  That framing is what made the miss mechanical -- the enumeration
638:#  asked which arms ANNOUNCE and treated a refusal as having no observable, when the refusal's
639:#  own returned value is exactly the channel M10 travels on.
640:t, alive = session(guest_store(A_SETISR, 0x5a5a) + guest_load(A_CLRISR))
641:row("isr 28 rd val", (printed_last(t, "v0") if alive else "died"), "0x0",
642:    "A declined READ at 0x10028 returns ZERO, not a mirror of d->isr -- the sibling of "
643:    "`isr 20 rd val'.  Seed via 0x10020 so the ISR is NON-ZERO: against a zero ISR the mirror "
644:    "mutant and the fix are indistinguishable, which is how a value row goes vacuous without "
645:    "looking it.  `kill 28 rd txt' stays GREEN under that mutant (the announce still fires), "
646:    "so no other row in this probe can see it.")
647:
648:#  =====================================================================================
649:#  THE LATCH-AXIS CLASS CLOSURE.  Obligation 5 of the fable5 adjudication, 2026-09-18.
650:#
651:#  *** THE LATCH IS NOT A SHARED RESOURCE WITH TWO FAILURE MODES.  It is one
652:#  `d->warned |= IP30_W_*' beside EVERY announce -- the same duplication the ANNOUNCE axis was
653:#  decomposed for, and leaving it atomic is how six mutants swept a 27-row instrument. ***
654:#
655:#  THE SPACE, enumerated from the function's own inputs:
656:#      {4 announce sites} x {announce fires, latch sets}  +  {2 collision directions} = TEN.
657:#  It was FOURTEEN before the F13 silent-decline repair removed the two READ-arm announces --
658:#  resolving that design question REMOVED four cells of detector work rather than adding them.
659:#
660:#  ALREADY CLOSED (6): the four announce rows (kill 20 wr txt, kill 28 wr txt, kill 30 txt,
661:#  tick 50 txt), the 0x10020-WR latch-set (latch 1x), and the TICK->ISR collision (xbit).
662:#  CLOSED HERE (4): the remaining three latch-set cells and the reverse collision direction.
663:#
664:#  *** THE SECOND TICK IS FORCEABLE, AND THAT WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. ***  The
665:#  adjudicator allowed an UNBUILDABLE cell to be disclosed with its apparatus reason, but only
666:#  after being measured unbuildable.  MEASURED, and the artifact is named because a cited
667:#  measurement must live at a durable path -- regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/
668:#  tickcount_20260918.out, sentinel TICKCOUNT_DONE, REPLICATED on an independent re-run.
669:#  Counting ticks by the unconditional `reg_0x20000 += 1000':
670:#        step 1 / 2 / 3 / 8 / 1000 -> ONE tick      step 65536 / 70000 -> TWO
671:#        step 140000 -> THREE
672:#  *** STOPPING AT `step 8' WOULD HAVE DISCLOSED TWO BUILDABLE CELLS AS UNBUILDABLE. ***  The
673:#  threshold is ~65536 because machine.c decrements ticks_till_next by ONE per single-step.
674:#  So these rows are SLOW by construction, not by accident; that is the price of the cell.
675:#  =====================================================================================
676:
677:#  Closes MB/MF/MG-shaped mutants at the two remaining ISR write arms: delete that arm's
678:#  `d->warned |= IP30_W_ISR' and it announces on EVERY access instead of once.
679:for _nm, _a in (("latch 28wr", A_CLRISR), ("latch 30wr", A_ISR)):
680:    t, alive = session(guest_store(_a, 0x1) + guest_store(_a, 0x1))
681:    row(_nm, (str(counted(t, ISRMSG)) if alive else "died"), "1",
682:        "TWO accesses at THIS ARM ALONE, so the count attributes to it.  `latch 1x' cannot: it "
683:        "visits 0x10020 then 0x10028 and the FIRST access latches, leaving the second arm's "
684:        "latch-set never load-bearing -- which is why MF_nolatchset28rd escaped 27/27.")
685:
686:#  Closes ME_nolatchsettick -- the worst of the eight: one guest `sd' arming imask0 bit 50
687:#  would otherwise give AN ANNOUNCE PER TICK, FOREVER.  Guest-drivable floodclass.
688:t, alive = session(guest_store64(A_IMASK0, 1 << 50) + ["step 65536"])
689:row("latch tick", (str(counted(t, TICKMSG)) if alive else "died"), "1",
690:    "*** TWO TICKS IN ONE LIFETIME (step 65536 -- MEASURED to fire exactly two). ***  Delete the "
691:    "tick's `d->warned |= IP30_W_TICK' and this reads 2, then 3, then forever.  No single-tick "
692:    "row can see it: with one tick the latched and unlatched builds are identical.")
693:
694:#  Closes MA_assign20wr -- the ISR->TICK collision direction, which `xbit' runs BACKWARDS.
695:t, alive = session(guest_store64(A_IMASK0, 1 << 50) + ["step 1"]
696:                   + guest_store(A_SETISR, 0x1) + ["step 65536"])
697:row("xbit rev", (str(counted(t, TICKMSG)) if alive else "died"), "1",
698:    "*** TICK -> ISR -> TICK: the order the product comment's own hazard needs, and the "
699:    "OPPOSITE of `xbit'. ***  Under `|=' -> `=' at the ISR arm the assignment CLOBBERS the tick "
700:    "bit, so the second tick announces again and this reads 2.  `xbit' runs tick->ISR and stops, "
701:    "so it cannot see the clobber -- which is why MA, a ONE-CHARACTER edit, scored 27/27.")
702:
703:#  *** THE MIRROR OF `xbit rev', AND ITS ABSENCE WAS MEASURED: V2_assigntick SCORED 32/32
704:#  AGAINST A CLOSURE THAT CLAIMED BOTH COLLISION DIRECTIONS. ***
705:#
706:#  A CLOBBER CAN ONLY BITE IF THE CLOBBERED CLASS'S BIT WAS ALREADY SET.  `xbit isr' runs
707:#  TICK -> ISR, so when the tick assigns, the ISR bit has never been set, nothing is lost, and
708:#  the row is green on the mutant.  Seeing it needs ISR FIRST:
709:#
710:#      ISR announces -> warned = IP30_W_ISR
711:#      tick ASSIGNS  -> warned = IP30_W_TICK   <- the ISR bit is LOST
712:#      ISR again     -> the guard passes again -> TWO announces where one is correct
713:#
714:#  `xbit rev' is the same idea with the operands swapped (ISR clobbers TICK).  Building one
715:#  direction and not its mirror is the #499 shape -- repaired at the address the mutant used,
716:#  symmetric cell left open -- and it is the SAME defect the opus5 seat diagnosed in the
717:#  ORIGINAL `xbit', reproduced inside the repair for it.
718:#
719:#  The 65536 steps are load-bearing: the tick must fire BETWEEN the two ISR accesses, and the
720:#  first tick of a session is consumed by the first `run_ops'.  Measured, not assumed.
721:t, alive = session(guest_store(A_SETISR, 0x1)
722:                   + guest_store64(A_IMASK0, 1 << 50) + ["step 65536"]
723:                   + guest_store(A_SETISR, 0x1))
724:row("xbit rev2", (str(counted(t, ISRMSG)) if alive else "died"), "1",
725:    "*** ISR -> TICK -> ISR: the mirror of `xbit rev', and the only order in which a TICK-side "
726:    "assignment can be seen to clobber the ISR bit. ***  Correct build announces ONCE (the "
727:    "latch holds across the tick); make the tick's latch set an ASSIGNMENT and the second ISR "
728:    "access announces again, reading 2.  `xbit isr' runs tick-first and is structurally blind "
729:    "to it -- V2_assigntick scored 32/32 against it.")
730:
731:
732:# ------------------------------------------------------------------ report
733:CONTROL = "OK"
734:for nm, got, want, _ in rows:
735:    if nm in ("live", "devsig") and got != want:
736:        CONTROL = "DEAD"
737:
738:print("ip30_abort_probe -- binary=%s label=%s machine=sgi/ip30" % (BIN, LABEL))
739:print("IP30ABORT_CONTROL=%s" % CONTROL)
740:if CONTROL == "DEAD":
741:    print("IP30ABORT_RESULT=0/0")
742:    print("  *** SETUP FAILED -- the vehicle is not the vehicle. NOT 'no defect'. ***")
743:    for nm, got, want, note in rows:
744:        print("  %-12s got=%-12s want=%-12s %s" % (nm, got, want, note[:60]))
745:    print("IP30ABORT_DONE")
746:    raise SystemExit(0)
747:
748:n = 0
749:for nm, got, want, note in rows:
750:    ok = (got == want)
751:    n += 1 if ok else 0
752:    print("  %-12s got=%-12s want=%-12s %s  %s" % (nm, got, want, "ok" if ok else "FAIL", note[:70]))
753:print("IP30ABORT_RESULT=%d/%d" % (n, len(rows)))
754:print("IP30ABORT_DONE")

